      <div type="commentary">
        <p n="1" ana="#tr.print">The framed manuscript page number [ⲕⲁ] is printed with a bar above and below as the frame, not as a supralinear stroke. Two specks at the right end of the line (x 1327 and 1379, 2 px each) are far smaller than the printed dots and are taken as dirt in the scan.</p>
        <p n="5" ana="#tr.bracket">Lines 4–11 each begin with an opening bracket that is not closed; on ll. 6–11 it marks the lost beginning of the line, and the braced statement (ll. 5–7 "zerstört", ll. 8–11 "unlesbar") describes the rest. Line 5 keeps ⲟⲡ after the bracket.</p>
        <p n="14" ana="#tr.bracket">The bracket before the last eight dots is not closed in the edition; it is closed here at the line end. So also l. 31 (four dots), l. 34 (three dots) and l. 35 (four dots).</p>
        <p n="18" ana="#tr.bracket">The final ⲉ is restored in brackets at the right edge, after the seven illegible dots, as at the end of l. 20.</p>
        <p n="19" ana="#tr.print">An enlarged ⲧ begins [ϯⲡⲉ] inside the line. Measured stroke over ϩⲙ (770–795): it covers ϩ by 56 % and ⲙ by 70 %, so it is encoded on both.</p>
        <p n="21" ana="#tr.print">Measured overline over ⲙⲛ (930–950): ⲙ and ⲛ touch in print; from the zoom it covers about 55 % of ⲙ and 50 % of ⲛ, so it is encoded on both.</p>
        <p n="23" ana="#tr.reading">ⲙⲯⲩⲭ[ⲁⲩⲉ]: the letter after ⲙ is printed with arms curved up (ⲯ), as on p. 20 l. 21.</p>
      </div>
